var e={id:"ncph",level:"core",title:"Presinusoidal portal hypertension",minutes:8,objectives:["Recognize portal hypertension with preserved liver function: varices, splenomegaly, normal liver stiffness","Explain why the HVPG is near normal in presinusoidal resistance","Name the two main causes: schistosomiasis and porto-sinusoidal vascular disorder (PSVD)"],checks:[["Name the two main causes of presinusoidal portal hypertension.","Schistosomiasis and porto-sinusoidal vascular disorder (PSVD)."],["Why is the HVPG near normal?","The resistance is presinusoidal, so the WHVP reflects normal sinusoidal pressure. Only a PPG, from direct portal pressure measurement, shows the gradient."],["Varices with normal liver stiffness. What does that suggest?","Presinusoidal or prehepatic resistance. Liver stiffness reflects fibrosis and congestion, not portal pressure."],["After a variceal bleed, liver stiffness is 6 kPa, the HVPG 4 mmHg and the portal vein patent on Doppler. Where is the resistance?","Presinusoidal, in the portal venules: the WHVP reflects the normal sinusoids downstream of them. Think of PSVD or schistosomiasis."]],summary:"Schistosomiasis and porto-sinusoidal vascular disorder (PSVD): varices and splenomegaly with normal liver stiffness and a normal HVPG, because the resistance is in the portal tracts.",slides:[{id:"patient",preset:"schisto",cam:"fit",terms:["varix","spleen"],normalOutline:["spleen"],calipers:["spleen"],data:"tiles",tiles:["varix","spleen","plt"],key:["varix"],kicker:"The patient",site:"presin",title:"Varices with normal liver function",vignette:"A 27-year-old woman who grew up near Lake Victoria in Uganda is admitted with hematemesis. She has never had jaundice, and she does not drink alcohol.",line:"A young adult with varices of {varix}, a spleen length of {spleen} and platelets of {plt}, but no jaundice, no ascites and normal liver tests.",notes:"This is the typical presentation of noncirrhotic portal hypertension: a first variceal bleed, or incidental splenomegaly, with preserved liver function. The bleed is usually well tolerated because hepatic reserve is preserved. Worldwide, schistosomiasis is a leading cause; where it is absent, porto-sinusoidal vascular disorder is the most common intrahepatic cause.",ask:["A patient has variceal bleeding, normal liver tests and no ascites. What should you consider?","Noncirrhotic portal hypertension: prehepatic or presinusoidal resistance."]},{id:"tract",lens:"drop",cam:"lobule:triad",callout:{at:"triad",label:"Resistance"},terms:{"portal venules":"lobule:triad",sinusoids:"lobule:sinusoid"},data:"tiles",tiles:["pv","sin"],key:["pv"],whatIf:{label:"Periportal fibrosis",param:["fibrosis.R.pre","fibrosis.L.pre"],min:1,max:80,step:1,ends:["None","Severe"]},kicker:"Site of resistance",site:"presin",title:"The resistance is in the portal tracts",line:"Schistosome eggs lodge in the portal venules; granulomas and fibrosis narrow them, while the sinusoids downstream stay at {sin}.",notes:"Adult Schistosoma mansoni and S. japonicum live in the mesenteric veins; their eggs embolize to the liver and lodge in the portal venules. Granulomas form around them and heal as periportal (pipestem) fibrosis. The sinusoids and hepatocytes are spared, which is why liver function is preserved and ascites is rare.",ask:["Why is liver function preserved for years in schistosomiasis?","The resistance is in the portal venules; the sinusoids and hepatocytes are spared."]},{id:"wedge",cath:"result",tool:{kind:"profile"},key:["pv","whvp","hvpg"],kicker:"Site of resistance",site:"presin",title:"The WHVP reflects normal sinusoidal pressure",line:"The resistance is presinusoidal, so the HVPG is {hvpg}. Only direct portal pressure measurement shows the gradient: a PPG of {ppg}.",notes:"In presinusoidal resistance the HVPG is normal or only mildly elevated and underestimates portal pressure, often by a wide margin. A normal HVPG in a patient with varices or splenomegaly points to a presinusoidal or prehepatic cause. Portal pressure can be measured directly, at TIPS or with an EUS-guided needle.",ask:["Varices and a 17 cm spleen; Doppler shows a patent portal vein. At transjugular hemodynamic study the HVPG is 3 mmHg. What do you do before the catheter is removed?","Perform a transjugular liver biopsy: clinically evident portal hypertension with a normal HVPG points to a presinusoidal cause such as PSVD."]},{id:"eus",procedure:"eus",cam:"liver",tool:{kind:"reading"},data:"tiles",tiles:["hvpg","ppg"],key:["ppg"],kicker:"Site of resistance",site:"presin",title:"EUS-PPG measures portal pressure directly",line:"An EUS-guided needle passed through the stomach measures the portal and hepatic vein pressures. Here the EUS-PPG is {ppg}, while the HVPG was {hvpg}.",notes:"Press Next to step through it: the echoendoscope in, the portal vein, the hepatic vein, and the gradient. A linear echoendoscope in the proximal stomach images the left lobe; a needle crosses into the left portal vein, then into the left hepatic vein, each measured with a manometer, and EUS-PPG is the portal vein pressure minus the hepatic vein pressure, from one needle in one session. Because it measures the portal vein directly, it detects the presinusoidal resistance the HVPG misses. The value of EUS-guided portal pressure gradient measurement in PSVD or noncirrhotic portal fibrosis (NCPF) is still a research question for Baveno VIII (RA8.18, RA8.24), so it is not a standard test; it is done in expert centers under sedation. The model does not change: the measurement reads the vessels as they are.",ask:["Why can EUS-PPG detect portal hypertension that the HVPG missed?","It measures the portal vein itself, upstream of the presinusoidal resistance; the WHVP reflects only sinusoidal pressure."]},{id:"eus-ladder",lens:"drop",terms:["lobule:triad"],cam:"lobule:triad",callout:{at:"triad",label:"Resistance"},tool:{kind:"profile",spans:["hvpg","eus","ppg"]},key:["hvpg","eus"],kicker:"Site of resistance",site:"presin",title:"The EUS-PPG spans the resistance that the wedge leaves out",line:"The HVPG is {hvpg:0}. The EUS-PPG starts in the portal vein, upstream of the resistance, and reads {eus:0}, close to the PPG of {ppg:0}.",notes:"The same patient, with the three gradients side by side. The HVPG is the wedged minus the free hepatic venous pressure, so it spans the sinusoids only. The EUS-PPG is the left portal vein minus the left hepatic vein pressure, so it starts upstream of the portal tracts and spans the resistance; the PPG spans the portal vein to the IVC. Baveno VIII lists the EUS-guided gradient in PSVD and NCPF as a research question (RA8.18, RA8.24), so it is not a standard test.",ask:["The HVPG is 3 mmHg and the EUS-PPG 15 mmHg in the same patient. Where does the resistance lie?","Between the left portal vein and the wedge, in the portal tracts. The EUS-PPG spans it and the HVPG does not."]},{id:"stiff",cam:"liver",terms:["liver"],tool:{kind:"fibroscan"},data:"tiles",tiles:["lsm"],key:["lsm"],kicker:"Site of resistance",site:"presin",title:"Liver stiffness stays low despite high portal pressure",line:"Liver stiffness (LSM) reflects fibrosis and congestion, not portal pressure, so it stays at {lsm}, <10 kPa. Varices with a normal LSM point to a noncirrhotic cause.",notes:"LSM rises with fibrosis and with hepatic congestion, so it stays normal or only mildly elevated in presinusoidal and prehepatic disease. An LSM <10 kPa with clear signs of portal hypertension should prompt a search for a noncirrhotic cause. Spleen stiffness (SSM), where available, is high because it tracks portal pressure; Baveno VIII counts an SSM >40 kPa as a specific sign of portal hypertension here (8.44). The LSM and platelet rules that spare a screening EGD in cirrhosis do not apply in PSVD: screen with EGD at diagnosis unless the SSM is <40 kPa with a bilirubin <1 mg/dL (8.46, 8.47).",ask:["LSM 6 kPa with large varices. Does that rule out portal hypertension?","No: it argues against cirrhosis. Portal hypertension with a normal LSM points to a noncirrhotic cause."]},{id:"psvd",preset:"psvd",cam:"lobule:triad",terms:{"portal venules":"lobule:triad"},data:"tiles",tiles:["hvpg","lsm"],kicker:"Another patient",site:"presin",title:"Porto-sinusoidal vascular disorder narrows the portal venules",vignette:"A 34-year-old man who has never left Europe has splenomegaly and a platelet count of 76,000/\xB5L. His liver tests are normal, his liver stiffness is 6 kPa, and he takes no medications.",line:"In another patient, without schistosomiasis, the same picture comes from obliterative portal venopathy: the portal venules narrow and disappear. Liver biopsy makes the diagnosis.",causesHead:"Associated with",causes:["Immune disorders","Thiopurines, oxaliplatin","HIV, didanosine","Thrombophilia"],notes:"Porto-sinusoidal vascular disorder replaces older names such as idiopathic noncirrhotic portal hypertension and hepatoportal sclerosis. It is associated with immune disorders and immunodeficiency, to thiopurines, oxaliplatin and didanosine, to HIV and to thrombophilia. The biopsy shows no cirrhosis, with obliterative portal venopathy, nodular regenerative hyperplasia or incomplete septal fibrosis. This is a different patient because hepatosplenic schistosomiasis excludes the diagnosis (Baveno VIII Table S2). As in schistosomiasis, the HVPG and LSM underestimate the severity of portal hypertension. Portal vein thrombosis (PVT) can coexist (8.40), so Doppler ultrasound is advised every 6 months once there are signs of portal hypertension (8.50).",ask:["How is porto-sinusoidal vascular disorder diagnosed?","An adequate liver biopsy without cirrhosis, plus a specific sign of portal hypertension (varices, collaterals), or a specific lesion (obliterative portal venopathy, nodular regenerative hyperplasia, incomplete septal cirrhosis), or one nonspecific sign with one nonspecific lesion."]},{id:"path-psvdscope",cam:"liver",glow:["liver"],tool:{kind:"path",of:"psvdscope",short:"EGD screening",patient:{bili:.8,v:"small"}},kicker:"Another patient",site:"presin",title:"In PSVD, spleen stiffness decides who needs screening EGD",line:"A spleen stiffness of {ssm} calls for EGD at diagnosis. Only {<40 kPa} with a bilirubin {<1 mg/dL} spares it; the cirrhosis LSM and platelet rules do not apply.",notes:"In porto-sinusoidal vascular disorder (PSVD) and noncirrhotic portal fibrosis (NCPF) the liver stiffness and platelet rules that spare a screening EGD in cirrhosis must not be used (Baveno VIII 8.48). Screening for gastroesophageal varices is advised at diagnosis (8.46). It can be omitted, at diagnosis and in follow-up, only when the spleen stiffness is <40 kPa and the bilirubin is <1 mg/dL (<17 \xB5mol/L), because varices are then unlikely (8.47); a patient at exactly 40 kPa or 1 mg/dL is not spared. When EGD is done, it is repeated every 2 years if it shows no varices and every year if it shows small varices (8.46). In the model his spleen stiffness is about 60 kPa, so EGD is indicated regardless of the bilirubin. The bilirubin of 0.8 mg/dL and the small varices on the lower row are the slide's own assumptions, not the model's; the lower row gives only the follow-up after an EGD that showed no varices or small varices.",ask:["A patient with PSVD has a spleen stiffness of 35 kPa and a bilirubin of 1.3 mg/dL. Does he need screening EGD?","Yes. Omitting EGD requires both a spleen stiffness <40 kPa and a bilirubin <1 mg/dL."]},{id:"path-psvdscore",cam:"lobule:triad",glow:["lobule:triad"],tool:{kind:"path",of:"psvdscore",short:"Diagnostic score",patient:{sg:0,hi:1,ac:1,cc:1}},kicker:"Another patient",site:"presin",title:"A score of 5 makes the diagnosis",line:"A spleen stiffness of {ssm} is a specific sign, scoring 3. A minor histologic lesion adds 2: a total of {>=5 points} makes the diagnosis definite.",notes:"This is the diagnostic score of Baveno VIII (Fig. 4, 8.42 to 8.44). It applies only when cirrhosis has been ruled out on an adequate liver biopsy (a specimen of 15 mm or more with a fragment of 10 mm or more, or one an expert pathologist finds adequate, 8.41) and no exclusion criterion is present. Clinical signs of portal hypertension score 3 if specific (medium or large esophageal varices, gastric varices, spontaneous portosystemic collaterals, or a spleen stiffness >40 kPa without a myeloproliferative neoplasm), 2 if nonspecific (clinical ascites, small esophageal varices, platelets <150,000/\xB5L, splenomegaly) and 0 if there is none. Histology scores 5 for a major lesion (nodular regenerative hyperplasia, muscularized portal venules, or stenosis of the portal venules in 50% or more of the portal tracts), 2 for a minor lesion (an abnormal distribution of the vascular structures, stenosis in 25% to 49% of the tracts, or regenerative changes without clear nodules) and 0 if normal; all three minor criteria together count as a major one (8.43). A condition known to be associated with PSVD adds 1, and a concomitant cause of cirrhosis subtracts 1. A total of 5 or more is a definite PSVD or NCPF; 3 or 4 is possible, which calls for further tests such as a repeat biopsy or elastography, referral to an expert center in vascular liver disease and follow-up for new signs. On the slide the model gives the spleen stiffness; the biopsy and the associated conditions are the case's own facts.",ask:["A patient has small varices and platelets of 120,000/\xB5L, and his biopsy shows a major lesion. Is the diagnosis definite?","Yes. Nonspecific signs score 2 and a major lesion 5, a total of 7, which is \u22655. A score of 3 or 4 would be only possible."]},{id:"psvd-care",cam:"liver",glow:["liver"],data:"tiles",tiles:["ssm","plt"],key:["ssm"],tool:{kind:"criteria",title:"Management of PSVD",short:"PSVD care",ticks:!1,lead:"After the diagnosis.",rule:"Baveno VIII 8.12, 8.13, 8.49, 8.53",rows:["Variceal bleeding: **managed** as in cirrhosis","**TIPS** for recurrent or refractory bleeding","**Cross-sectional imaging** of the portal venous system at diagnosis","**Liver transplant** for severe, refractory complications"],verdict:"Every 6 months: Doppler to screen for PVT (8.50)."},kicker:"Another patient",site:"presin",title:"Variceal bleeding in PSVD is managed as in cirrhosis",line:"If he bleeds, acute management and secondary prophylaxis follow the cirrhosis guidelines. TIPS is for recurrent or refractory bleeding, and liver transplant for severe complications.",notes:"Baveno VIII 8.12: in vascular liver disease with acute portal hypertensive bleeding, the acute management and the secondary prophylaxis follow the guidelines for cirrhosis. 8.8 and 8.9: there is no evidence to favor endoscopic variceal ligation over a beta-blocker, or carvedilol over another nonselective beta-blocker. 8.13: TIPS should be considered for refractory or recurrent bleeding. 8.14: there are no data for preemptive TIPS. 8.49: at diagnosis, contrast-enhanced cross-sectional imaging shows the portal veins, any collaterals or hepatic veno-venous communications and any nodules. 8.50: with signs of portal hypertension, Doppler ultrasound every 6 months screens for PVT. 8.53: consider liver transplant for severe, refractory complications of portal hypertension, hepatopulmonary syndrome, HCC or advanced liver dysfunction, discussed in a transplant center with vascular liver disease expertise.",ask:["A man with PSVD has recurrent variceal bleeding despite a beta-blocker and band ligation. What comes next?","TIPS should be considered for recurrent or refractory bleeding (Baveno VIII 8.13)."]},{id:"relation",preset:"schisto",cam:"fit",terms:["varix","spleen"],tool:{kind:"curve",x:"lsm",xCuts:[10],patient:{preset:"healthy"},param:"cirrhosis",from:0,to:1,step:.1,days:365,lines:["ppg","spleen"],dots:[{preset:"csph",name:"Cirrhosis, CSPH"}]},kicker:"Summary",site:"presin",title:"Liver stiffness and the PPG rise together only in cirrhosis",line:"Along the line, cirrhosis raises LSM, PPG and spleen length together. The schistosomiasis patient has a PPG of {ppg} and a spleen of {spleen}, yet an LSM of only {lsm}.",notes:"The line is the model's cirrhosis from none to severe, each point settled for a year: LSM on the x axis, the PPG and the spleen length against it. In cirrhosis the three rise together; the point marks cirrhosis with CSPH. The ring is the schistosomiasis patient: a PPG of 16 mmHg and a spleen of 17 cm at an LSM of 6 kPa, where the cirrhosis line has a PPG of about 5 mmHg and a spleen of about 11 cm. The resistance is presinusoidal, so LSM stays normal while the pressure upstream of the portal tracts climbs. PSVD gives the same picture. The dashes mark 10 kPa: an LSM <10 kPa with signs of portal hypertension points to vascular liver disease. (Baveno VIII 1.6; EASL 2025: LSM normal or only mildly elevated in PSVD and chronic PVT.) One model patient per point; real patients vary far more.",ask:["A patient has large varices, a 17 cm spleen and an LSM of 6 kPa. Is that cirrhosis?","No. The LSM is far below the cirrhosis line. Portal hypertension with a normal LSM points to presinusoidal or prehepatic resistance."]},{id:"summary",visual:"table",cols:["pv","hvpg","ppg","lsm","spleen"],asc:!1,rowHead:"Patient",of:[{preset:"healthy",kicker:"Reference",title:"Healthy",ref:!0},{id:"patient",kicker:"Presinusoidal",title:"Schistosomiasis"},{preset:"csph",kicker:"Sinusoidal",title:"Cirrhosis with CSPH"}],kicker:"Summary",title:"Presinusoidal resistance versus cirrhosis",line:"Both have a high PPG and splenomegaly. Only cirrhosis raises the HVPG and liver stiffness.",notes:"Suspect a noncirrhotic cause when signs of portal hypertension come with normal liver tests, a normal or near-normal LSM and a low HVPG. Image the portal venous system, take a travel and medication history, and obtain a liver biopsy. Variceal bleeding is managed as in cirrhosis (Baveno VIII 8.12), and TIPS should be considered for recurrent or refractory bleeding (8.13).",ask:["Which two tests are falsely reassuring in presinusoidal portal hypertension?","The HVPG and the liver stiffness."]}]};export{e as a};
